<http://data.silknow.org/object/10a0b30a-cd9b-3098-98ee-43c872b01cd5>
        a                   ecrm:E73_Information_Object ;
        rdfs:comment        "The Japanese typically use a number of embroidery stitches to create varied and realistic motifs. In these fukusa  the artist has used couching to create the scales and raised lips of the fish and satin stitch to create a smooth surface for the eyes. Traditionally, sea creatures are related to the many stages of life and are symbols of strength in the face of opposition." , "The Applied Image" , "March 11 - June 21, 1998" ;
        ecrm:P129_is_about  <http://data.silknow.org/object/81ca3dbc-fc5a-385e-8ec6-5ef58dac796b> ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    "Exhibition" ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "March 11 - June 21, 1998" , "The Applied Image" , "The Japanese typically use a number of embroidery stitches to create varied and realistic motifs. In these fukusa  the artist has used couching to create the scales and raised lips of the fish and satin stitch to create a smooth surface for the eyes. Traditionally, sea creatures are related to the many stages of life and are symbols of strength in the face of opposition." ;
        schema:contentUrl   <https://risdmuseum.org/exhibitions-events/exhibitions/applied-image> .

